/* King Solomon: 30-case judgment series data. Generated by tools/gen-solomon.js
   from the original King Solomon app sources (adult/KJV cases). Texts are never
   reworded. Do not hand-edit. */
VG.register({
id:"king-solomon",
title:"King Solomon",
kicker:"KJV \u00b7 Proverbs in the court",
source:"1 Kings 3 \u00b7 the court of Solomon",
desc:"Sit as judge in Solomon\u2019s court. Hear each cause, choose among three judgments, and let the proverbs train your ear. Scroll One is gentle; from Scroll Two a wrong judgment bears its cost.",
meta:"3 scrolls \u00b7 30 cases \u00b7 judge each cause",
shelf:"mix",
mode:"solomon",
playable:true,
art:"img/card-king-solomon.webp",
scrolls:[{"name":"The first scroll · Cases of the court","cases":[{"id":1,"title":"The Two Mothers","setting":"The throne room at dusk","kind":"SCRIPTURE","reference":"1 Kings 3:16–28","story":"Two women stood before Solomon with one living child. Each claimed to be his mother, and each accused the other of exchanging the children after one infant died in the night. No witness could settle the matter. The testimony was perfectly divided. Solomon needed a judgment that would expose what words concealed.","question":"What is your judgment?","consequence":"","image":"img/solomon-case-01.webp","alt":"Solomon’s lamplit throne room at dusk","options":[{"text":"Call for a sword and announce that the child will be divided. Watch which woman would rather surrender him than see him harmed.","correct":1,"feedback":"This is the judgment Solomon gave. The true mother pleaded that the living child be given to the other woman. Her compassion exposed the truth, and Solomon gave her the child unharmed.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 20:5","quote":"Counsel in the heart of man is like deep water; but a man of understanding will draw it out."}},{"text":"Give the child to the woman who spoke first, because the first account is usually strongest.","correct":0,"feedback":"A first telling can sound complete while still being false. A judge must hear and test both sides.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 18:17","quote":"He that is first in his own cause seemeth just; but his neighbour cometh and searcheth him."}},{"text":"Send both women away until one returns with a witness.","correct":0,"feedback":"That avoids the hard work of judgment. Wisdom draws out what is hidden when witnesses are unavailable.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 18:13","quote":"He that answereth a matter before he heareth it, it is folly and shame unto him."}}]},{"id":2,"title":"The Merchant’s Measure","setting":"A crowded stall after sunset","kind":"STORY","reference":"Original story","story":"A grain merchant was accused of buying with heavy stones and selling with light ones. He insisted that every stone bore the market seal. The accuser could not identify which exchange had been dishonest, but two sets of nearly identical weights lay beneath the counter.","question":"What is your judgment?","consequence":"","image":"img/solomon-case-02.webp","alt":"Bound barley sheaves across a merchant’s grain field at sunset","options":[{"text":"Test both sets against the court standard, repay everyone cheated, and remove the false weights.","correct":1,"feedback":"The court must test the instrument, not merely weigh the confidence of the speaker. Honest measures protect the whole market.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 11:1","quote":"A false balance is abomination to the LORD: but a just weight is his delight."}},{"text":"Trust the merchant because official seals always prove honesty.","correct":0,"feedback":"A mark can be copied. Prudence tests a claim instead of believing every word.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 14:15","quote":"The simple believeth every word: but the prudent man looketh well to his going."}},{"text":"Fine both the merchant and the accuser so no one argues in public again.","correct":0,"feedback":"Punishing both sides without learning the facts is a quick answer, not a just one.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 18:13","quote":"He that answereth a matter before he heareth it, it is folly and shame unto him."}}]},{"id":3,"title":"The Sealed Jar","setting":"A potter’s bench at first light","kind":"STORY","reference":"Original story","story":"A master potter and a celebrated painter disputed ownership of a sealed ceremonial jar. One supplied the clay and kiln, the other the design and pigments. Each described an oral agreement that gave him the finished vessel. Their apprentices had heard only pieces of the bargain.","question":"What is your judgment?","consequence":"","image":"img/solomon-case-03.webp","alt":"A sealed painted jar with brushes and pigments on a potter’s bench at first light","options":[{"text":"Hear each worker separately, question the apprentices, then decide what the original agreement required.","correct":1,"feedback":"The first account may sound convincing. Wisdom searches it by hearing the other side and testing the details.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 18:17","quote":"He that is first in his own cause seemeth just; but his neighbour cometh and searcheth him."}},{"text":"Award the jar to the more famous craftsman.","correct":0,"feedback":"Reputation is not evidence. A prudent judge looks carefully before choosing.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 14:15","quote":"The simple believeth every word: but the prudent man looketh well to his going."}},{"text":"Break the jar and divide the pieces equally.","correct":0,"feedback":"A fast answer can destroy the very good entrusted to judgment. Hear the whole matter first.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 18:13","quote":"He that answereth a matter before he heareth it, it is folly and shame unto him."}}]},{"id":4,"title":"The Quarreling Wells","setting":"Two courtyards in the heat","kind":"STORY","reference":"Original story","story":"Two householders shared an underground water channel. During drought, one well fell sharply while the other remained full. Accusations of night theft became threats. The old boundary record was unclear, and both families had gathered outside the court.","question":"What is your judgment?","consequence":"","image":"img/solomon-case-04.webp","alt":"Still water and green hills beneath a clearing dawn sky","options":[{"text":"Quiet the room, inspect the channel, and speak gently while arranging a fair water schedule.","correct":1,"feedback":"A calm answer opens space for facts and keeps anger from becoming a second dispute.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 15:1","quote":"A soft answer turneth away wrath: but grievous words stir up anger."}},{"text":"Let the louder neighbor speak until the other gives up.","correct":0,"feedback":"Noise is not proof. Answering before hearing the whole matter is folly.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 18:13","quote":"He that answereth a matter before he heareth it, it is folly and shame unto him."}},{"text":"Order each family to repay whatever it believes the other took.","correct":0,"feedback":"Private revenge multiplies harm. The court should restrain it and pursue peace.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 20:22","quote":"Say not thou, I will recompense evil; but wait on the LORD, and he shall save thee."}}]},{"id":5,"title":"The Torn Ledger","setting":"A treasury room by lamplight","kind":"STORY","reference":"Original story","story":"A treasury clerk entered a payment under the wrong household, discovered it, and corrected the record before funds moved. A rival copied the torn draft and circulated it as proof of corruption. The error was real. The accusation of theft was not.","question":"What is your judgment?","consequence":"","image":"img/solomon-case-05.webp","alt":"A torn papyrus ledger with reed pens and counting stones on a treasury table by lamplight","options":[{"text":"Confirm the correction, clear the clerk of theft, and stop the rival from repeating an accusation already settled.","correct":1,"feedback":"Justice can name a corrected fault without turning it into a weapon. Covering an offense in this sense protects love, while repetition divides.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 17:9","quote":"He that covereth a transgression seeketh love; but he that repeateth a matter separateth very friends."}},{"text":"Publish the torn draft across the market so everyone can decide.","correct":0,"feedback":"Repeating a settled matter can separate friends and stain a name beyond the facts.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 17:9","quote":"He that covereth a transgression seeketh love; but he that repeateth a matter separateth very friends."}},{"text":"Dismiss both clerks immediately, before hearing either one.","correct":0,"feedback":"Haste feels decisive, but judgment without a hearing brings folly and shame.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 18:13","quote":"He that answereth a matter before he heareth it, it is folly and shame unto him."}}]},{"id":6,"title":"The Cracked Wall","setting":"A vineyard before the storm","kind":"STORY","reference":"Original story","story":"A retaining wall above the southern road showed fresh cracks after weeks of rain. Its owner refused closure because carts bound for his vineyard would be delayed. A mason testified that another storm could send stone across the road, but no collapse had yet occurred.","question":"What is your judgment?","consequence":"","image":"img/solomon-case-06.webp","alt":"A stone wall under repair beneath gathering rain clouds","options":[{"text":"Close the road for a short time and require the wall to be strengthened before the storm.","correct":1,"feedback":"Prudence acts when danger can be seen. It does not wait for preventable harm to become proof.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 22:3","quote":"A prudent man foreseeth the evil, and hideth himself: but the simple pass on, and are punished."}},{"text":"Keep the road open until a stone actually falls.","correct":0,"feedback":"The simple pass on after seeing danger and suffer for it. Waiting is also a choice.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 22:3","quote":"A prudent man foreseeth the evil, and hideth himself: but the simple pass on, and are punished."}},{"text":"Destroy the whole vineyard immediately.","correct":0,"feedback":"Wisdom prevents danger without creating needless ruin. First learn what repair truly requires.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 18:13","quote":"He that answereth a matter before he heareth it, it is folly and shame unto him."}}]},{"id":7,"title":"The Caravan’s Promise","setting":"A road gate beneath a red sky","kind":"STORY","reference":"Original story","story":"A caravan master took advance payment for dyed cloth and guaranteed arrival by the new moon. He later claimed storms caused the delay. Gate records showed clear weather, while a stable keeper said the caravan had stopped to pursue a more profitable sale.","question":"What is your judgment?","consequence":"","image":"img/solomon-case-07.webp","alt":"A caravan road winding toward a distant city beneath a scroll shaped cloud","options":[{"text":"Question the gatekeeper and stable keeper, then require the trader to tell the truth and repair the loss caused by his false promise.","correct":1,"feedback":"A lie may win a moment, but truth is what endures. Judgment should restore the buyer and establish honest speech.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 12:19","quote":"The lip of truth shall be established for ever: but a lying tongue is but for a moment."}},{"text":"Accept the storm story because the trader told it confidently.","correct":0,"feedback":"Confidence does not make a report true. Prudence examines the road before it believes the traveler.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 14:15","quote":"The simple believeth every word: but the prudent man looketh well to his going."}},{"text":"Confiscate every caravan at the gate from now on.","correct":0,"feedback":"One man’s lie does not justify careless judgment against everyone. Hear each matter fully.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 18:13","quote":"He that answereth a matter before he heareth it, it is folly and shame unto him."}}]},{"id":8,"title":"The Borrowed Ox","setting":"A farm track under moonlight","kind":"STORY","reference":"Original story","story":"A farmer lent an ox for plowing. The borrower exceeded the agreed time, worked the animal after it began limping, and returned it injured. The owner then seized the borrower’s donkey in retaliation. Each man now demanded punishment for the other.","question":"What is your judgment?","consequence":"","image":"img/solomon-case-08.webp","alt":"An injured ox and tethered donkey beside a broken harness on a moonlit farm track","options":[{"text":"Return the donkey, require the borrower to pay for the ox’s care and lost work, and forbid either man from taking revenge.","correct":1,"feedback":"Justice repairs the proven wrong without blessing retaliation. Wisdom refuses to repay evil with more evil.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 20:22","quote":"Say not thou, I will recompense evil; but wait on the LORD, and he shall save thee."}},{"text":"Let the farmer keep the donkey because revenge makes the debt even.","correct":0,"feedback":"Taking repayment into your own hands easily creates a second injustice.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 20:22","quote":"Say not thou, I will recompense evil; but wait on the LORD, and he shall save thee."}},{"text":"Return every animal and make no one answer for the injured ox.","correct":0,"feedback":"Mercy does not erase responsibility. A righteous person regards the life of an animal.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 12:10","quote":"A righteous man regardeth the life of his beast: but the tender mercies of the wicked are cruel."}}]},{"id":9,"title":"The Widow’s Offering","setting":"A palace corridor before worship","kind":"STORY","reference":"Original story","story":"A palace official moved a boundary marker and absorbed a widow’s strip of olive trees into his estate. When accused, he pointed to a costly public offering and his gifts to the city. The survey stones and two old records supported the widow.","question":"What is your judgment?","consequence":"","image":"img/solomon-case-09.webp","alt":"Storm clouds opening above a solitary figure seeking justice","options":[{"text":"Restore the widow’s land and require the official to repay its fruit. His offering cannot replace justice.","correct":1,"feedback":"God values righteousness and justice more than an impressive sacrifice used to hide wrongdoing.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 21:3","quote":"To do justice and judgment is more acceptable to the LORD than sacrifice."}},{"text":"Let the official keep the land because his public gift helped many people.","correct":0,"feedback":"A generous display cannot purchase permission to wrong one neighbor.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 21:3","quote":"To do justice and judgment is more acceptable to the LORD than sacrifice."}},{"text":"Delay the case until the widow can bring a richer offering too.","correct":0,"feedback":"Justice is not sold to the person who can give the most. Hear the evidence, not the gifts.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 18:17","quote":"He that is first in his own cause seemeth just; but his neighbour cometh and searcheth him."}}]},{"id":10,"title":"The Cedar Contract","setting":"The council chamber at sunrise","kind":"STORY","reference":"Original story","story":"Two builders sought a royal contract before the autumn rains. The lower bid concealed inferior timber and omitted drainage work from its tally. The higher bid included seasoned cedar, repairs to the channels, and a schedule witnessed by the guild. Both accused the other of greed.","question":"What is your judgment?","consequence":"","image":"img/solomon-case-10.webp","alt":"Strong cedar beams, weak warped boards, a contract, and builders’ tools at sunrise","options":[{"text":"Examine the timber, the full tally, and the guild’s testimony. Choose the sound plan, then publish the measure used to judge both bids.","correct":1,"feedback":"Wisdom draws counsel from beneath the surface. A low number alone is not the whole case.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 20:5","quote":"Counsel in the heart of man is like deep water; but a man of understanding will draw it out."}},{"text":"Choose the lowest price immediately and ask no further questions.","correct":0,"feedback":"The first offer may look right until another is examined. Wise judgment searches both claims.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 18:17","quote":"He that is first in his own cause seemeth just; but his neighbour cometh and searcheth him."}},{"text":"Choose whichever builder praises the king most warmly.","correct":0,"feedback":"Pleasant words are not proof. The prudent person considers the path carefully.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 14:15","quote":"The simple believeth every word: but the prudent man looketh well to his going."}}]}]},{"name":"The second scroll · Training in Proverbs","cases":[{"id":11,"title":"The Adviser’s Lamp","setting":"The eastern palace gate at blue hour","kind":"STORY","reference":"Original story","story":"A favored adviser accused the old lamplighter of stealing palace oil after the eastern gate went dark. He praised the throne’s discernment and urged immediate punishment to calm the guard. The oil register was smudged, one sealed jar remained uncounted, and a sentry recalled lending the adviser a lamp after dusk.","question":"Which counsel deserves the court’s trust?","consequence":"The lamplighter is dismissed. By midnight three gates are dark, and a servant who told the truth has lost his bread.","image":"img/solomon-case-11.webp","alt":"An old lamplighter’s bronze lamp and records at a palace gate","options":[{"text":"Separate praise from proof. Examine the jar, the register, and the sentry before judging either man.","correct":1,"feedback":"Flattery can feel like loyalty while drawing the judge into another person’s net. Wisdom tests the claim.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 29:5","quote":"A man that flattereth his neighbour spreadeth a net for his feet."}},{"text":"Accept the adviser’s charge because a trusted servant protects the throne’s honor.","correct":0,"feedback":"This sounds orderly, but it protects appearance while leaving the central wrong untested.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 29:5","quote":"A man that flattereth his neighbour spreadeth a net for his feet."}},{"text":"Dismiss the case quietly because public inquiry would embarrass the palace.","correct":0,"feedback":"This notices a real danger, but answers it with another injustice instead of careful judgment.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 29:5","quote":"A man that flattereth his neighbour spreadeth a net for his feet."}}]},{"id":12,"title":"The Gleaner’s Boundary","setting":"A barley field beneath a storm gold sky","kind":"STORY","reference":"Original story","story":"After harvest, a landowner shifted ancient boundary stones and claimed the standing corners where poor families gathered grain. His deed described the former markers, yet his steward argued that the new line improved security and therefore served the estate. Three households who had gleaned there for years stood at the gate.","question":"What should the court protect first?","consequence":"The families lose the grain they depended on. The steward moves another stone before dawn because the court rewarded the first theft.","image":"img/solomon-case-12.jpg","alt":"A weary gleaner gathers barley beside a moved field boundary beneath a slate storm sky","options":[{"text":"Restore the old boundary, hear the families, and forbid the landowner from using wealth to close the gate against them.","correct":1,"feedback":"A neat settlement can still reward oppression. The poor must not be robbed because they lack power at the gate.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 22:22","quote":"Rob not the poor, because he is poor: neither oppress the afflicted in the gate:"}},{"text":"Honor the new line because a landowner must control every part of his field.","correct":0,"feedback":"This sounds orderly, but it protects appearance while leaving the central wrong untested.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 22:22","quote":"Rob not the poor, because he is poor: neither oppress the afflicted in the gate:"}},{"text":"Divide the disputed grain equally, but let the moved stones remain for peace.","correct":0,"feedback":"This notices a real danger, but answers it with another injustice instead of careful judgment.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 22:22","quote":"Rob not the poor, because he is poor: neither oppress the afflicted in the gate:"}}]},{"id":13,"title":"The Beloved Governor","setting":"The purple council chamber before dawn","kind":"STORY","reference":"Original story","story":"A governor’s nephew violated the market ordinance that had already cost a poorer family its stall. The governor recited years of public works and argued that strict punishment would weaken a useful household. His supporters filled the chamber, while the displaced merchant waited outside.","question":"How should past service affect this judgment?","consequence":"The poorer family watches the nephew keep the stall. The crowd learns that one law serves the powerful and another serves everyone else.","image":"img/solomon-case-13.webp","alt":"A royal crown beside an intricate screen in a purple council chamber","options":[{"text":"Thank the governor for true service, then apply the same measure to his nephew and restore the displaced family.","correct":1,"feedback":"Favor and overcorrection both bend the measure around a person. Wisdom judges the deed without respecting faces.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 24:23","quote":"These things also belong to the wise. It is not good to have respect of persons in judgment."}},{"text":"Reduce the penalty because the governor’s long service has earned his household special mercy.","correct":0,"feedback":"This sounds orderly, but it protects appearance while leaving the central wrong untested.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 24:23","quote":"These things also belong to the wise. It is not good to have respect of persons in judgment."}},{"text":"Punish the nephew twice as severely to prove that the court cannot be influenced.","correct":0,"feedback":"This notices a real danger, but answers it with another injustice instead of careful judgment.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 24:23","quote":"These things also belong to the wise. It is not good to have respect of persons in judgment."}}]},{"id":14,"title":"The Sealed Well","setting":"The lower village at first light","kind":"STORY","reference":"Original story","story":"A wealthy owner bought the parcel surrounding the lower village well, then sealed its mouth and demanded payment for every jar. The deed granted the soil but said nothing of the water. Village families had rebuilt the stonework after each flood, and the owner now called their labor a gift to his estate.","question":"What does justice require when law and duty meet?","consequence":"Children spend the day walking to a distant spring. One family sells its seed grain just to buy water from the sealed well.","image":"img/solomon-case-14.webp","alt":"A cracked village well with water jars and boundary stones at dawn","options":[{"text":"Open the well to the families who maintained it, then settle fair care of the land without withholding water from those due it.","correct":1,"feedback":"A legal power is not permission to withhold a good that is plainly due. Justice corrects the wrong without committing another.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 3:27","quote":"Withhold not good from them to whom it is due, when it is in the power of thine hand to do it."}},{"text":"Enforce the deed exactly as the owner reads it because clear ownership prevents disorder.","correct":0,"feedback":"This sounds orderly, but it protects appearance while leaving the central wrong untested.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 3:27","quote":"Withhold not good from them to whom it is due, when it is in the power of thine hand to do it."}},{"text":"Take the entire parcel from the owner because his demand was cruel.","correct":0,"feedback":"This notices a real danger, but answers it with another injustice instead of careful judgment.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 3:27","quote":"Withhold not good from them to whom it is due, when it is in the power of thine hand to do it."}}]},{"id":15,"title":"The Mason’s Loophole","setting":"An unfinished city wall at sunset","kind":"STORY","reference":"Original story","story":"A master mason demanded final payment because every numbered course in the contract stood complete. An apprentice warned that the hidden rain braces had never been fitted. The master replied that the schedule mentioned support but never named that kind of brace, and the first storm was gathering beyond the ridge.","question":"Should the court accept the narrow wording?","consequence":"The payment is released. That night a wet section bows over the road, and the apprentice is injured while warning travelers away.","image":"img/solomon-case-15.webp","alt":"An unfinished city wall and scaffolding beneath a gathering storm","options":[{"text":"Delay approval, inspect the wall, and require the safety work before the storm reaches the city.","correct":1,"feedback":"Prudence sees danger before it arrives. It neither worships a loophole nor destroys sound work without examining it.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 22:3","quote":"A prudent man foreseeth the evil, and hideth himself: but the simple pass on, and are punished."}},{"text":"Approve payment because the written list is complete and courts must not add words to contracts.","correct":0,"feedback":"This sounds orderly, but it protects appearance while leaving the central wrong untested.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 22:3","quote":"A prudent man foreseeth the evil, and hideth himself: but the simple pass on, and are punished."}},{"text":"Order the wall torn down at once because any missing work proves the whole structure false.","correct":0,"feedback":"This notices a real danger, but answers it with another injustice instead of careful judgment.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 22:3","quote":"A prudent man foreseeth the evil, and hideth himself: but the simple pass on, and are punished."}}]},{"id":16,"title":"The Festival Lamp","setting":"The festival court above the valley","kind":"STORY","reference":"Original story","story":"A merchant presented a costly festival offering while twelve laborers petitioned for wages he had withheld. He argued that the public gift brought greater honor to the city than twelve private payments, and asked the court to count his sacrifice as settlement of their claims.","question":"Which act carries greater weight before the court?","consequence":"The festival shines while twelve homes go without bread. The merchant’s gift is praised, and unpaid labor becomes easier to hide.","image":"img/solomon-case-16.webp","alt":"A bronze oil lamp glowing above a blue valley before the festival","options":[{"text":"Require every wage to be paid. A public offering cannot replace righteousness and justice toward the workers.","correct":1,"feedback":"Ceremony cannot purchase release from justice. The workers must receive what is theirs, without forbidding later repentance and generosity.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 21:3","quote":"To do justice and judgment is more acceptable to the LORD than sacrifice."}},{"text":"Accept the gift as payment because a sacred offering benefits more people than private wages.","correct":0,"feedback":"This sounds orderly, but it protects appearance while leaving the central wrong untested.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 21:3","quote":"To do justice and judgment is more acceptable to the LORD than sacrifice."}},{"text":"Reject the gift and ban the merchant from ever giving again.","correct":0,"feedback":"This notices a real danger, but answers it with another injustice instead of careful judgment.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 21:3","quote":"To do justice and judgment is more acceptable to the LORD than sacrifice."}}]},{"id":17,"title":"The Whispered Petition","setting":"The gate forge after midnight","kind":"STORY","reference":"Original story","story":"A court messenger privately warned three judges that a captain intended treason. One detail was true: the captain had met a foreign courier beyond the gate. The messenger withheld his source, embellished the hour at each telling, and called secrecy proof that he was protecting the kingdom.","question":"How should the court handle a partly true secret?","consequence":"The captain is seized before a hearing. His soldiers abandon the gate in confusion, while the real courier disappears with the unanswered truth.","image":"img/solomon-case-17.webp","alt":"A dark gate forge glowing with sparks after midnight","options":[{"text":"Stop the whispering, preserve the claim, and test the messenger’s source and changing details in a proper hearing.","correct":1,"feedback":"A talebearer can carry a true fragment inside a destructive story. Wisdom neither spreads it nor refuses to investigate it.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 20:19","quote":"He that goeth about as a talebearer revealeth secrets: therefore meddle not with him that flattereth with his lips."}},{"text":"Reward the messenger because dangerous news must travel quickly even when its source stays hidden.","correct":0,"feedback":"This sounds orderly, but it protects appearance while leaving the central wrong untested.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 20:19","quote":"He that goeth about as a talebearer revealeth secrets: therefore meddle not with him that flattereth with his lips."}},{"text":"Ignore every part of the report because one changing detail makes all of it false.","correct":0,"feedback":"This notices a real danger, but answers it with another injustice instead of careful judgment.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 20:19","quote":"He that goeth about as a talebearer revealeth secrets: therefore meddle not with him that flattereth with his lips."}}]},{"id":18,"title":"The Orchard Witness","setting":"An olive wall after rain","kind":"STORY","reference":"Original story","story":"A farmer brought tablets recording the olives lost when his neighbor’s tree bent across the boundary during a storm. His account was orderly, and the damaged wall seemed to prove it. Under questioning, a child described the farmer driving a loaded cart against the same wall before the rain, and fresh wheel cuts remained in the mud.","question":"What should the court do with a convincing first account?","consequence":"The neighbor pays for the entire wall and loses the tree. The farmer hides his own damage, and the child learns that a polished story defeats the truth.","image":"img/solomon-case-18.webp","alt":"An olive tree bent over a broken orchard wall after rain","options":[{"text":"Hear the neighbor, inspect the wheel cuts, and separate the damage caused by the cart from the damage caused by the tree.","correct":1,"feedback":"The first account may seem just, and a discovered omission may tempt the opposite extreme. Searching both claims reveals the measured truth.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 18:17","quote":"He that is first in his own cause seemeth just; but his neighbour cometh and searcheth him."}},{"text":"Rule for the farmer because documents and visible damage make his first account complete.","correct":0,"feedback":"This sounds orderly, but it protects appearance while leaving the central wrong untested.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 18:17","quote":"He that is first in his own cause seemeth just; but his neighbour cometh and searcheth him."}},{"text":"Rule for the neighbor because the hidden cart marks prove the farmer lied about everything.","correct":0,"feedback":"This notices a real danger, but answers it with another injustice instead of careful judgment.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 18:17","quote":"He that is first in his own cause seemeth just; but his neighbour cometh and searcheth him."}}]},{"id":19,"title":"The Silent Apprentice","setting":"A caravan road outside the western gate","kind":"STORY","reference":"Original story","story":"A young apprentice witnessed a guild master threaten a caravaner into sealing a false debt. At the hearing, guild custom gave masters the first voice and treated apprentices as dependents without standing. The accused master praised the court’s respect for tradition and moved to silence the only direct witness.","question":"Does court custom settle who may be heard?","consequence":"The false debt stands. The traveler loses his caravan, and every apprentice in the guild learns that truth has no voice against rank.","image":"img/solomon-case-19.webp","alt":"A caravan road leading toward the western city gate","options":[{"text":"Open the apprentice’s mouth in the cause, protect the witness, and test the testimony with every other fact.","correct":1,"feedback":"Wisdom speaks for those who cannot secure a hearing, then judges their words honestly. Giving a voice is not abandoning proof.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 31:8","quote":"Open thy mouth for the dumb in the cause of all such as are appointed to destruction."}},{"text":"Honor guild order and hear only masters, because stable courts must preserve established voices.","correct":0,"feedback":"This sounds orderly, but it protects appearance while leaving the central wrong untested.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 31:8","quote":"Open thy mouth for the dumb in the cause of all such as are appointed to destruction."}},{"text":"Accept the apprentice’s word without testing it because silenced people are always correct.","correct":0,"feedback":"This notices a real danger, but answers it with another injustice instead of careful judgment.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 31:8","quote":"Open thy mouth for the dumb in the cause of all such as are appointed to destruction."}}]},{"id":20,"title":"The Balanced Ledger","setting":"The treasury archive beneath golden light","kind":"STORY","reference":"Original story","story":"The royal treasurer presented accounts whose columns balanced to the last measure. A broken seal revealed a side tablet recording grain sent to a storehouse absent from every city map. He argued that opening private memoranda after the official sum had balanced would destroy confidence in faithful officers.","question":"When the numbers agree, should the search end?","consequence":"The false storehouse receives another convoy. By harvest’s end, grain meant for hungry districts has vanished behind perfect totals.","image":"img/solomon-case-20.webp","alt":"Sealed treasury scrolls, scales, and a false invoice in golden lamplight","options":[{"text":"Search out the hidden storehouse, compare every seal, and honor the office by bringing the whole matter into light.","correct":1,"feedback":"A king’s honor is not protected by stopping at a tidy surface. It searches the concealed matter without condemning everyone before proof.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 25:2","quote":"It is the glory of God to conceal a thing: but the honour of kings is to search out a matter."}},{"text":"Close the inquiry because balanced totals are the court’s strongest proof of honest service.","correct":0,"feedback":"This sounds orderly, but it protects appearance while leaving the central wrong untested.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 25:2","quote":"It is the glory of God to conceal a thing: but the honour of kings is to search out a matter."}},{"text":"Seize every treasury officer’s property before learning who made the side tablet.","correct":0,"feedback":"This notices a real danger, but answers it with another injustice instead of careful judgment.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 25:2","quote":"It is the glory of God to conceal a thing: but the honour of kings is to search out a matter."}}]}]},{"name":"The third scroll · Wisdom in the Gates","cases":[{"id":21,"title":"The River Tribute","setting":"The throne room above the river provinces","kind":"STORY","reference":"Original story","story":"Three river provinces send the same tribute, though floods ruined one harvest and doubled another province’s trade. The chief collector proposes a new levy based on each governor’s private gift to the crown. He calls it flexible, since generous governors would receive lighter assessments and the treasury would be filled at once. His ledgers hide which payments are tribute and which are gifts.","question":"How should the king establish the tribute?","consequence":"The flooded province sells its seed grain to pay. Wealthy governors purchase lighter burdens, and every collector learns that a gift can bend the king’s measure.","image":"img/solomon-case-21.webp","alt":"A king hears a tribute dispute before the throne","options":[{"text":"Set a public measure tied to each province’s true capacity, audit every ledger, and forbid private gifts from altering tribute.","correct":1,"feedback":"Justice establishes the land only when the measure is visible and gifts cannot purchase a different rule.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 29:4","quote":"The king by judgment establisheth the land: but he that receiveth gifts overthroweth it."}},{"text":"Keep equal tribute for every province because one number looks impartial and protects the treasury from political pressure.","correct":0,"feedback":"Equal figures can hide unequal burdens. This protects the appearance of order while ignoring what each province can bear.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 29:4","quote":"The king by judgment establisheth the land: but he that receiveth gifts overthroweth it."}},{"text":"Allow private gifts to reduce a governor’s levy, provided every gift is recorded and the total revenue still rises.","correct":0,"feedback":"A recorded gift can still purchase judgment. Transparency does not make a bent measure straight.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 29:4","quote":"The king by judgment establisheth the land: but he that receiveth gifts overthroweth it."}}]},{"id":22,"title":"The House Before Mourning","setting":"The estate chamber beside an empty throne","kind":"STORY","reference":"Original story","story":"A governor dies while his eldest son is abroad. Before the burial, a younger son presents a copy of the estate seal and asks to divide the vineyards at once. He argues that speed will prevent servants from stealing and that custom favors the son who remains. A steward reports that the governor’s final inventory is still sealed with the city scribe, and a daughter holds letters promising care for two dependent households.","question":"Should the estate be divided now?","consequence":"The vineyards are transferred before the true inventory is opened. Two households are expelled, hidden debts surface, and the brothers spend years fighting over land already sold.","image":"img/solomon-case-22.webp","alt":"A royal council weighs a contested estate inheritance","options":[{"text":"Secure the estate, open the final inventory with all heirs present, settle its duties and debts, then divide what truly remains.","correct":1,"feedback":"Haste can make possession look like inheritance. Wisdom protects the estate until truth, duty, and every lawful claim are known.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 20:21","quote":"An inheritance may be gotten hastily at the beginning; but the end thereof shall not be blessed."}},{"text":"Divide the land now under the younger son’s seal, then correct any mistake after the absent heir returns.","correct":0,"feedback":"This seems practical, but land sold in haste may not be recoverable when the missing claims appear.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 20:21","quote":"An inheritance may be gotten hastily at the beginning; but the end thereof shall not be blessed."}},{"text":"Give the estate to the eldest son alone because rank prevents rivalry and preserves the governor’s house.","correct":0,"feedback":"Rank can settle order without erasing recorded duties, debts, or the claims of others.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 20:21","quote":"An inheritance may be gotten hastily at the beginning; but the end thereof shall not be blessed."}}]},{"id":23,"title":"The Governor’s Reports","setting":"The bronze gate of the northern market","kind":"STORY","reference":"Original story","story":"A governor reports that new tolls have brought peace to the northern market. Every officer beneath him repeats the claim. Yet merchants arrive with stamped receipts showing different charges for the same cart, and a dismissed clerk says the governor rewards reports that praise his policy. The governor offers to remove one collector while keeping the reporting system untouched.","question":"Where must the king’s inquiry begin?","consequence":"One collector is sacrificed and the false reports continue. Honest clerks are driven out, tolls rise in secret, and the throne receives only the words it has trained officials to say.","image":"img/solomon-case-23.webp","alt":"Merchants and officers dispute revenue at a crowded city gate","options":[{"text":"Examine what the governor rewards, protect truthful witnesses, compare receipts, and reform the whole chain of reporting.","correct":1,"feedback":"A ruler shapes the speech beneath him. If lies win favor at the top, replacing one servant leaves the corruption rooted.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 29:12","quote":"If a ruler hearken to lies, all his servants are wicked."}},{"text":"Remove the collector named by the governor because swift discipline will warn the rest without weakening public confidence.","correct":0,"feedback":"A visible punishment may calm the market while protecting the system that taught officers to lie.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 29:12","quote":"If a ruler hearken to lies, all his servants are wicked."}},{"text":"Accept the united testimony of the officers because agreement across many reports is stronger than scattered merchant complaints.","correct":0,"feedback":"Agreement is weak evidence when every speaker is rewarded for repeating the same lie.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 29:12","quote":"If a ruler hearken to lies, all his servants are wicked."}}]},{"id":24,"title":"The Closed War Council","setting":"The rain darkened eastern gate","kind":"STORY","reference":"Original story","story":"Scouts report movement beyond the eastern ridge. The king’s celebrated captain urges an immediate march through the narrow pass, arguing that secrecy is the soul of surprise. He asks the king to exclude the quartermaster, border governor, and older commander because more voices may slow the decision. The captain’s victories are real, but he has not seen the flooded ford or counted provisions for a second route.","question":"How should the king decide before sending the army?","consequence":"The army enters the pass without enough grain or a safe crossing. Supply carts sink at the ford, soldiers are trapped between two ridges, and border villages stand unguarded.","image":"img/solomon-case-24.webp","alt":"Guards and petitioners gather at a rain swept city gate","options":[{"text":"Hear the captain, quartermaster, governor, and veteran together, test the routes and supplies, then commit the army with one clear command.","correct":1,"feedback":"Many counselors do not mean endless delay. Their tested knowledge keeps one gifted voice from carrying the kingdom into unseen danger.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 24:6","quote":"For by wise counsel thou shalt make thy war: and in multitude of counsellors there is safety."}},{"text":"Follow the captain alone because proven victories give him the best claim to judge a changing battlefield.","correct":0,"feedback":"Past victory matters, but it does not supply the facts held by those who know the ford, stores, and border.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 24:6","quote":"For by wise counsel thou shalt make thy war: and in multitude of counsellors there is safety."}},{"text":"Delay every movement until all advisers agree, since any disagreement proves that the plan is unsafe.","correct":0,"feedback":"Counsel should expose risk, not make unanimity a substitute for decision and command.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 24:6","quote":"For by wise counsel thou shalt make thy war: and in multitude of counsellors there is safety."}}]},{"id":25,"title":"The Debt of Two Nations","setting":"The lower treasury beneath the citadel","kind":"STORY","reference":"Original story","story":"A neighboring kingdom owes silver for grain received during famine. Its new ruler cannot pay without seizing next season’s seed, yet your treasury borrowed that silver from merchant houses to fund the same relief. The merchants propose control of the debtor’s river customs until every coin returns. The envoy offers half the debt now if the rest is erased, though his palace still imports cedar and horses.","question":"What settlement protects both justice and freedom?","consequence":"The merchants take the river customs without limits. Fees consume the smaller kingdom’s trade, farmers remain servants to the debt, and resentment turns relief into lasting domination.","image":"img/solomon-case-25.webp","alt":"A treasury steward opens disputed chests before a royal inspector","options":[{"text":"Audit the debtor’s true capacity, stop luxury spending, set a bounded repayment from trade, and keep the river customs under public oversight.","correct":1,"feedback":"Debt creates real obligation and real power. Wisdom orders repayment without turning temporary need into permanent rule.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 22:7","quote":"The rich ruleth over the poor, and the borrower is servant to the lender."}},{"text":"Grant the merchants full control of customs because those who supplied the silver should choose the strongest security.","correct":0,"feedback":"The debt is real, but unlimited control can make repayment a doorway to rule rather than restoration.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 22:7","quote":"The rich ruleth over the poor, and the borrower is servant to the lender."}},{"text":"Erase the debt entirely because grain saved lives and mercy should not be measured in silver.","correct":0,"feedback":"Mercy matters, but canceling a lawful debt without hearing those who funded the grain shifts the burden to another innocent party.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 22:7","quote":"The rich ruleth over the poor, and the borrower is servant to the lender."}}]},{"id":26,"title":"The Judge’s Son","setting":"The high court beside the treasury scales","kind":"STORY","reference":"Original story","story":"The chief judge’s son is accused of diverting relief silver through a false supplier. The accounts carry his seal, but the judge argues that enemies often forge noble marks. He proposes acquitting his son to protect the court’s authority while condemning the clerk who entered the payment, since the clerk admits handling the tablet but denies knowing the supplier was false.","question":"How should the king judge when guilt and innocence cross ranks?","consequence":"The son returns to office and the clerk is condemned. Witnesses stop coming forward, stolen relief silver disappears, and every magistrate sees that rank can turn guilt into innocence.","image":"img/solomon-case-26.webp","alt":"A royal debt hearing unfolds beside scales and ledgers","options":[{"text":"Remove the judge from the hearing, test the seal and payment trail, convict only the proven offender, and clear anyone the evidence does not condemn.","correct":1,"feedback":"Justice cannot preserve its name by reversing guilt and innocence. Rank must neither excuse the wicked nor burden the just.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 17:15","quote":"He that justifieth the wicked, and he that condemneth the just, even they both are abomination to the LORD."}},{"text":"Acquit the son unless a witness saw him take the silver, because a court must protect its officers from political accusations.","correct":0,"feedback":"Protecting officers from rumor is sound, but it cannot erase documents, payments, and evidence that can be tested.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 17:15","quote":"He that justifieth the wicked, and he that condemneth the just, even they both are abomination to the LORD."}},{"text":"Condemn both men because each touched the false payment and shared responsibility preserves public confidence.","correct":0,"feedback":"Shared contact is not shared guilt. Condemning the innocent beside the guilty does not restore confidence in justice.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 17:15","quote":"He that justifieth the wicked, and he that condemneth the just, even they both are abomination to the LORD."}}]},{"id":27,"title":"The Seven Sealed Granaries","setting":"The western granaries during a lean harvest","kind":"STORY","reference":"Original story","story":"The royal steward has sealed seven public granaries after a poor harvest. He says withholding grain now will raise its value, attract foreign silver, and protect next year’s reserves. Bakers answer that two stores already hold more than the planting reserve, while the poor queue at the gates. A merchant guild offers to buy the whole surplus and release it slowly at a higher price.","question":"What is faithful stewardship of the public grain?","consequence":"The guild buys the surplus behind closed doors. Bread prices triple, children go hungry beside full storehouses, and the people curse both merchant and throne.","image":"img/solomon-case-27.webp","alt":"A steward guards a chained granary during scarcity","options":[{"text":"Verify the planting reserve, publish the store counts, and sell the true surplus fairly before scarcity becomes a weapon.","correct":1,"feedback":"A reserve protects tomorrow, but hoarding surplus from hungry people corrupts stewardship into profit by suffering.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 11:26","quote":"He that withholdeth corn, the people shall curse him: but blessing shall be upon the head of him that selleth it."}},{"text":"Keep every granary sealed until prices peak because greater revenue will let the crown help more people later.","correct":0,"feedback":"Future aid sounds prudent, but it is purchased by deepening the present hunger the grain was stored to relieve.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 11:26","quote":"He that withholdeth corn, the people shall curse him: but blessing shall be upon the head of him that selleth it."}},{"text":"Release all grain without measure because public stores belong to the people in any season of want.","correct":0,"feedback":"Openhandedness without preserving seed may exchange today’s hunger for a greater famine next year.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 11:26","quote":"He that withholdeth corn, the people shall curse him: but blessing shall be upon the head of him that selleth it."}}]},{"id":28,"title":"The Sapphire Beneath the Robe","setting":"The grain market court inside the city gate","kind":"STORY","reference":"Original story","story":"Two merchants dispute the only license to supply grain to the army. One offers the lower price but has missed deliveries before. The other has stronger caravans and quietly places a sapphire beneath the presiding judge’s robe, calling it a gift of gratitude with no written condition. The judge proposes awarding the license to the stronger merchant without disclosing the stone, since the choice could be defended on merit.","question":"Can a sound result survive a hidden gift?","consequence":"The contract goes to the stronger merchant. Soon every bidder brings a hidden gift, honest traders leave the gate, and the army pays whatever favored suppliers demand.","image":"img/solomon-case-28.webp","alt":"Grain merchants bargain before a judge in a crowded market court","options":[{"text":"Remove the judge, return and disclose the sapphire, then compare price, capacity, and delivery terms before an unbought court.","correct":1,"feedback":"A defensible result does not cleanse a secret bribe. Justice must be both sound in reason and free from purchased influence.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 17:23","quote":"A wicked man taketh a gift out of the bosom to pervert the ways of judgment."}},{"text":"Keep the judge and award the stronger bid because rejecting the best supplier over an unsolicited gift would harm the army.","correct":0,"feedback":"The supplier’s strength may be real, but secrecy makes it impossible to know whether merit or payment moved the court.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 17:23","quote":"A wicked man taketh a gift out of the bosom to pervert the ways of judgment."}},{"text":"Award the license to the cheaper merchant as a penalty for the other bidder’s gift, without examining past failures.","correct":0,"feedback":"Punishing bribery is right, but handing the army to an unreliable supplier creates another injustice instead of a clean hearing.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 17:23","quote":"A wicked man taketh a gift out of the bosom to pervert the ways of judgment."}}]},{"id":29,"title":"The Treaty of Reeds","setting":"The cedar council chamber above the western road","kind":"STORY","reference":"Original story","story":"A neighboring prince offers an alliance against raiders. His cavalry would double the border force, but twice he has abandoned smaller allies when their defense became costly. His envoy argues that those failures prove experience, not disloyalty, and offers hostages as security. Your own commanders warn that the prince controls the only pass by which aid could arrive.","question":"What should the king build upon this promise?","consequence":"The army trusts the promised cavalry and leaves the second pass thinly guarded. The prince withdraws when battle turns, and the border breaks where his strength was counted but never came.","image":"img/solomon-case-29.webp","alt":"Rulers debate an alliance around a map in the council chamber","options":[{"text":"Do not rest the border on his promise. Keep independent defenses, test limited cooperation, and require proven faithfulness before deeper trust.","correct":1,"feedback":"A useful ally may still be an unfaithful support. Wisdom distinguishes cautious cooperation from confidence that carries the kingdom’s full weight.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 25:19","quote":"Confidence in an unfaithful man in time of trouble is like a broken tooth, and a foot out of joint."}},{"text":"Accept the alliance fully because hostages convert uncertain loyalty into an enforceable promise.","correct":0,"feedback":"Security can raise the cost of betrayal, but it cannot make absent cavalry arrive when danger comes.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 25:19","quote":"Confidence in an unfaithful man in time of trouble is like a broken tooth, and a foot out of joint."}},{"text":"Reject every agreement with the prince because a ruler who failed allies once can never serve any useful purpose.","correct":0,"feedback":"Past unfaithfulness limits trust, but guarded cooperation need not place the kingdom’s weight upon his word.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 25:19","quote":"Confidence in an unfaithful man in time of trouble is like a broken tooth, and a foot out of joint."}}]},{"id":30,"title":"Mercy at the Black Gate","setting":"The border camp before judgment","kind":"STORY","reference":"Original story","story":"A defeated city’s gatekeeper opened a postern gate to your army after its rulers began executing families who asked for peace. By law, every enemy officer who resisted the first summons faces death. The general demands the full penalty to keep military law plain. The gatekeeper admits serving the enemy until the final night, but his act spared the city from assault and delivered the murderers for trial.","question":"How should mercy and strict justice meet at the gate?","consequence":"The gatekeeper is executed under the broad rule. Other cities learn that surrender brings no measured mercy, so they fight longer, and families die behind gates that might have opened.","image":"img/solomon-case-30.webp","alt":"Commanders weigh justice and mercy in a border battle camp","options":[{"text":"Judge his earlier service truthfully, weigh the lives his final act saved, punish any proven crime, and extend measured mercy for faithful help.","correct":1,"feedback":"Mercy does not erase truth, and truth does not forbid mercy. Together they preserve authority better than a rule applied without distinction.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 20:28","quote":"Mercy and truth preserve the king: and his throne is upholden by mercy."}},{"text":"Apply death without exception because a law loses authority the first time a useful act changes its penalty.","correct":0,"feedback":"Consistency matters, but refusing to distinguish betrayal, repentance, and rescue can make law serve its wording instead of justice.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 20:28","quote":"Mercy and truth preserve the king: and his throne is upholden by mercy."}},{"text":"Grant full pardon and office because saving the city proves that every earlier act should be forgotten.","correct":0,"feedback":"Mercy can recognize his rescue without declaring every earlier deed innocent or making usefulness the price of office.","anchor":{"ref":"Proverbs 20:28","quote":"Mercy and truth preserve the king: and his throne is upholden by mercy."}}]}]}]
});
